// The arena: one opaque 256x256 background image, scrolled as a single layer.
//
// ── Why this is NOT `bg_bands`, even though it obviously should be ───────────────────────────────
// A fighting stage is horizontally stratified — sky, cliff, treeline, floor — which is exactly the
// shape per-scanline band parallax handles, and that is how this was first built: one layer, four
// bands, three depths for free (see examples/bands-demo).
//
// It shredded. `bg_bands` hands the horizontal scroll register to an HBlank DMA whose table is
// rebuilt from the camera inside `frame()`, and agb commits that frame WITHOUT waiting for vblank
// when the game has already overrun. On a late frame the DMA is therefore re-armed partway down the
// visible screen, its source pointer restarts at row 0, and every row below the re-arm gets the
// SKY band's offset instead of its own — the treeline jumps eight pixels sideways for the bottom
// half of the picture. Measured on this game: ~11,000 Timer2 ticks on its busiest frames against a
// 4,389-tick budget, so several frames a second were late, and each one was a visible tear.
//
// The lesson is not "bands are broken", it is that BANDS TURN A DROPPED FRAME INTO A CORRUPT ONE.
// They belong on a scene with headroom — bands-demo has a whole frame spare. A plain layer degrades
// the other way: its scroll register is written once, so a late commit shifts the whole picture by
// the one pixel the camera moved, which nobody can see.
//
// What is lost is depth, and less than it sounds: the stage is 256px against a 240px screen, so the
// camera has sixteen pixels of travel in the entire match.
import { bg_parallax } from 'cargo:tish_agb'

// How far apart the corners are, in world pixels. The camera's whole range is those 16 spare pixels;
// the walls are inset from the screen edge so a cornered fighter reads as against the wall rather
// than half off the side of it.
export let STAGE_LEFT: i32 = 30
export let STAGE_RIGHT: i32 = 226

// Half the camera's speed: enough that the backdrop is not nailed to the screen, slow enough that
// the arena floor does not visibly slide under the fighters' feet.
let DRIFT: i32 = 128

export function stageBands(bg: i32) {
  bg_parallax(bg, DRIFT, 0)
}
